# Tech Layoffs — Exploratory Data Analysis (SQL)

## Overview
This project explores global tech industry layoffs from 2020 to 2023 using SQL,
uncovering trends across companies, industries, countries, and time periods.
The dataset covers workforce reductions across hundreds of tech companies worldwide
during one of the most turbulent periods in the industry's history.

## Questions Explored
- Which companies laid off the most employees in total?
- Which industries and countries were hit hardest?
- How did layoffs trend over time month by month?
- Which companies laid off 100% of their workforce and how much had they raised?
- Who were the top 5 companies laying off the most people each year?

## What I Did
- Analyzed maximum layoff volume and percentage to identify worst-case scenarios
- Identified companies that completely shut down (100% laid off) ranked by
  funds raised — revealing which well-funded startups still failed
- Aggregated total layoffs by company, industry, country, and year
- Built a month-over-month rolling total to track cumulative layoff growth over time
- Used DENSE_RANK() with PARTITION BY to rank the top 5 companies by layoffs
  per year across the entire dataset
- Chained multiple CTEs to build a clean, layered analysis pipeline

## Key Findings
- Amazon, Google, Meta, Salesforce, and Microsoft ranked among the top companies
  by total layoffs — with Amazon leading at over 18,000 layoffs
- The Consumer and Retail industries were hit hardest by total headcount reductions
- The United States accounted for the majority of all layoffs in the dataset
  by a significant margin
- 2022 and 2023 saw the sharpest spike in layoffs — the rolling total showed
  exponential growth during this period driven by post-pandemic correction
  and rising interest rates
- Several companies that raised hundreds of millions in funding still laid off
  100% of their workforce — highlighting that capital raised does not guarantee survival
- Post-IPO stage companies accounted for the highest total layoffs, reflecting
  large workforce sizes and pressure from public market performance

## Tools
- MySQL
- MySQL Workbench

## Dataset
Global tech industry layoffs dataset (2020–2023)
Source: layoffs.fyi — tracking tech layoffs since COVID-19

## Skills Demonstrated
- Aggregate functions (SUM, MAX, AVG, MIN)
- GROUP BY with multiple dimensions
- Time-series analysis using SUBSTRING date parsing
- Rolling totals using SUM() OVER window functions
- DENSE_RANK() with PARTITION BY for year-over-year ranking
- Nested CTEs for multi-step analytical pipelines
- Exploratory data analysis (EDA) methodology
